{:edb/db-before-t 1876 :edb/db-after-t 1877 :edb/tx-data [[13194139535189 50 #inst "2026-10-08T19:05:32.276Z" 13194139535189 true] [17592186091902 1100 "A function $f(x)$ has values according to the table below:\n\n| $x$ | $2$ | $3$ | $4$ | $5$ | $6$ | $7$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $-1.0$ | $-0.7$ | $-0.3$ | $0.7$ | $1.5$ | $2.1$ |\n\nApproximate  at the point $x=3$ using a forward difference approximation." 13194139535189 true] [17592186091902 1100 "A function $f(x)$ has values according to the table below:\n\n| $x$ | $2$ | $3$ | $4$ | $5$ | $6$ | $7$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $f(x)$ | $-1.0$ | $-0.7$ | $-0.3$ | $0.7$ | $1.5$ | $2.1$ |\n\nApproximate at the point $x=3$ using a forward difference approximation." 13194139535189 false] [17592186091902 1145 "We use the forward difference approximation formula\n\n$$\nf'(x) \\approx \\frac{f(x+h) - f(x)}{h}\n$$\n\n \n\nat the point $x=3.$ We choose the step size $h$ to be as small as possible, in this case $h=1.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(3) & ≈\\frac{f(3+1)-f(3)}{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{f(4)-f(3)}{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{(-0.3)-(-0.7)}{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{0.4}{1} \\\\  & =0.4.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535189 true] [17592186091902 1145 "We use the forward difference approximation formula\n\n$$\nf'(x) \\approx \\frac{f(x+h) - f(x)}{h}\n$$\n\nat the point $x=3$. We choose the step size $h$ to be as small as possible, in this case $h=1$. This gives\n\n$$\nf^{′}(3) \\mid \\approx (f(3 + 1) - f(3))/(1); = (f(4) - f(3))/(1); = ((-0.3) - (-0.7))/(1); = \\frac{0.4}{1}; = 0.4\n$$" 13194139535189 false] [17592186091903 1100 "The graph of the function $y=g(x)$ is shown below.\nApproximate $g'(x)$ at the point $x=4$ using a forward difference approximation with step size $h=2$.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/992/Source/Images/f7601d421583e4e520cba5b7a0094276.png)" 13194139535189 false] [17592186091903 1100 "The graph of the function $y=g(x)$ is shown below. \nApproximate $g'(x)$ at the point $x=4$ using a forward difference approximation with step size $h=2.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14057748/assets/3084d6ae67f37517f3d8ad0a9a62be05fe1906fcc2a24f69bd29a1e00dfd0373.png)" 13194139535189 true] [17592186091903 1145 "We use the forward difference approximation\n\n$$\ng'(x) \\approx \\frac{g(x+h) - g(x)}{h}\n$$\n\n \n\nat the point $x=4.$  Applying the above formula with $x=4$ and $h=2$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}^{′}(4) & ≈\\frac{g(4+2)-g(4)}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{g(6)-g(4)}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{2-6}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{-4}{2} \\\\  & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535189 true] [17592186091903 1145 "We use the forward difference approximation\n\n$$\ng'(x) \\approx \\frac{g(x+h) - g(x)}{h}\n$$\n\nat the point $x=4$. Applying the above formula with $x=4$ and $h=2$ gives\n\n$$\ng^{′}(4) \\mid \\approx (g(4 + 2) - g(4))/(2); = (g(6) - g(4))/(2); = \\frac{2 - 6}{2}; = \\frac{-4}{2}; =-2\n$$" 13194139535189 false] [17592186091904 1100 "The position of a race car along a straight track, measured in feet from the starting line, is given by the function $s(t)$, where $t$ is the time in seconds. The values of $s(t)$ for different values of $t$ are given in the table below:\n\n| $t$ | $0$ | $0.1$ | $0.2$ | $0.3$ | $0.4$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $s(t)$ | $0$ | $3$ | $7$ | $12$ | $19$ |\n\nApproximate the velocity $s'(t)$ at the time $t=0.1$ using a forward difference approximation." 13194139535189 false] [17592186091904 1100 "The position of a race car along a straight track, measured in feet from the starting line, is given by the function $s(t),$ where $t$ is the time in seconds. The values of $s(t)$ for different values of $t$ are given in the table below:\n\n| $t$ | $0$ | $0.1$ | $0.2$ | $0.3$ | $0.4$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $s(t)$ | $0$ | $3$ | $7$ | $12$ | $19$ |\n\nApproximate the velocity $s'(t)$ at the time $t=0.1$ using a forward difference approximation." 13194139535189 true] [17592186091904 1145 "We use the forward difference approximation\n\n$$\ns'(t) \\approx \\frac{s(t+h) - s(t)}{h}\n$$\n\n \n\nat the time $t=0.1.$ We choose the step size $h$ to be as small as possible, in this case $h=0.1.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{s}^{′}(0.1) & ≈\\frac{s(0.1+0.1)-s(0.1)}{0.1} \\\\  & =\\frac{s(0.2)-s(0.1)}{0.1} \\\\  & =\\frac{7-3}{0.1} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{0.1} \\\\  & =40.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo at $t=0.1$ the velocity is approximately $40\\, \\textrm{ft}/\\textrm{s}.$" 13194139535189 true] [17592186091904 1145 "We use the forward difference approximation\n\n$$\ns'(t) \\approx \\frac{s(t+h) - s(t)}{h}\n$$\n\nat the time $t=0.1$. We choose the step size $h$ to be as small as possible, in this case $h=0.1$. This gives\n\n$$\ns^{′}(0.1) \\mid \\approx (s(0.1 + 0.1) - s(0.1))/(0.1); = (s(0.2) - s(0.1))/(0.1); = \\frac{7 - 3}{0.1}; = \\frac{4}{0.1}; = 40\n$$\n\nSo at $t=0.1$ the velocity is approximately $40\\, \\textrm{ft}/\\textrm{s}$." 13194139535189 false] [17592186091905 1100 "An oil tank has leaked $q(t)$ liters of oil after $t$ hours. The graph of $q(t)$ is shown below. Approximate the rate at which the oil is leaking from the tank when $t=1$ using a forward difference approximation with step size $h=1$.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/992/Source/Images/b42fd1d22af4ee74a06a5344ab52a8c8.png)" 13194139535189 false] [17592186091905 1100 "An oil tank has leaked $q(t)$ liters of oil after $t$ hours. The graph of $q(t)$ is shown below. Approximate the rate at which the oil is leaking from the tank when $t=1$ using a forward difference approximation with step size $h=1.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14057748/assets/b6c5286dae7e739a588173bad9c721ae34d07f86e455e44ec9f229628cf54211.png)" 13194139535189 true] [17592186091905 1145 "We use the forward difference approximation\n\n$$\nq'(t) \\approx \\frac{q(t+h) - q(t)}{h}\n$$\n\n \n\nat the time $t=1$ with step size $h=1.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{q}^{′}(1) & ≈\\frac{q(1+1)-q(1)}{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{q(2)-q(1)}{1} \\\\  & =3-2 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, after $1$ hour, the tank is leaking oil at a rate of approximately $1\\,\\textrm{liter}/ \\textrm{hour}.$" 13194139535189 true] [17592186091905 1145 "We use the forward difference approximation\n\n$$\nq'(t) \\approx \\frac{q(t+h) - q(t)}{h}\n$$\n\nat the time $t=1$ with step size $h=1$. This gives\n\n$$\nq^{′}(1) \\mid \\approx (q(1 + 1) - q(1))/(1); = (q(2) - q(1))/(1); = 3 - 2; = 1\n$$\n\nSo, after $1$ hour, the tank is leaking oil at a rate of approximately $1\\,\\textrm{liter}/ \\textrm{hour}$." 13194139535189 false] [17592186092333 1020 #edb/ref 17592186336481 13194139535189 true] [17592186092333 1020 #edb/ref 17592186336485 13194139535189 true] [17592186092333 1020 #edb/ref 17592186336488 13194139535189 true] [17592186092333 1020 #edb/ref 17592186336537 13194139535189 true] [17592186092333 1020 #edb/ref 17592186336544 13194139535189 true] [17592186092334 1020 #edb/ref 17592186336491 13194139535189 true] [17592186092334 1020 #edb/ref 17592186336523 13194139535189 true] [17592186092334 1020 #edb/ref 17592186336530 13194139535189 true] [17592186092334 1020 #edb/ref 17592186336551 13194139535189 true] [17592186092335 1020 #edb/ref 17592186336494 13194139535189 true] [17592186092335 1020 #edb/ref 17592186336498 13194139535189 true] [17592186092335 1020 #edb/ref 17592186336510 13194139535189 true] [17592186092335 1020 #edb/ref 17592186336558 13194139535189 true] [17592186092336 1020 #edb/ref 17592186336477 13194139535189 true] [17592186092336 1020 #edb/ref 17592186336501 13194139535189 true] [17592186092336 1020 #edb/ref 17592186336504 13194139535189 true] [17592186092336 1020 #edb/ref 17592186336507 13194139535189 true] [17592186092664 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139535189 true] [17592186092664 1100 "> A calculator is required to answer this question.\n\nThe function $g(s)$ describes the amount of fuel, in liters, remaining in Samuel's car after driving $s$ miles. The values of $g(s)$ for different values of $s$ are given in the table below:\n\n| $s$ | $0$ | $2$ | $4$ | $6$ | $8$ | $10$ | $12$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $g(s)$ | $15$ | $13.5$ | $12.1$ | $9.4$ | $8.2$ | $6.6$ | $4.8$ |\n\nApproximate the rate of change in the fuel volume when $s = 8$ miles using a forward difference approximation." 13194139535189 false] [17592186092664 1100 "The function $g(s)$ describes the amount of fuel, in liters, remaining in Samuel's car after driving $s$ miles. The values of $g(s)$ for different values of $s$ are given in the table below:\n\n| $s$ | $0$ | $2$ | $4$ | $6$ | $8$ | $10$ | $12$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $g(s)$ | $15$ | $13.5$ | $12.1$ | $9.4$ | $8.2$ | $6.6$ | $4.8$ |\n\nApproximate the rate of change in the fuel volume when $s=8$ miles using a forward difference approximation." 13194139535189 true] [17592186092664 1145 "We use the forward difference approximation\n\n$$\n{g}^{′}(s)≈\\frac{g(s+h)-f(s)}{h}\n$$\n\n \nat the point $s=8.$ We choose the step size $h$ to be as small as possible, in this case $h=2.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}^{′}(8) & ≈\\frac{g(8+2)-g(8)}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{g(10)-g(8)}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{6.6-8.2}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{-1.6}{2} \\\\  & =-0.8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo at $s=8$ the rate at which the fuel is changing is approximately $-0.8\\,\\text{liters/mile}.$" 13194139535189 true] [17592186092665 1017 #edb/ref 17592186090455 13194139535189 false] [17592186092665 1017 #edb/ref 17592186336517 13194139535189 true] [17592186092665 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139535189 true] [17592186092665 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14057748/assets/4a7e89a828cbd660357d4834e0bcb9cb5c316dbe24419865206ba5f41872963d.png)\n\nThe power of a mobile phone emitter increases when the phone is switched on. The power output of the emitter after $t$ minutes is given by $W(t),$ where $W$ is measured in watts. The graph of $W(t)$ is shown above. \nApproximate the rate of increase in the power of the emitter when $t=1$ using a forward difference approximation with step size $h=2.$" 13194139535189 true] [17592186092665 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/992/Source/Images/q-47383.png)\n\nThe power of a mobile phone emitter increases when the phone is switched on. The power output of the emitter after $t$ minutes is given by $W(t)$, where $W$ is measured in watts. The graph of $W(t)$ is shown above.\nApproximate the rate of increase in the power of the emitter when $t = 1$ using a forward difference approximation with step size $h = 2$." 13194139535189 false] [17592186092665 1145 "We use the forward difference approximation\n\n$$\n{W}^{′}(t)≈\\frac{W(t+h)-W(t)}{h},\n$$\n\n \nat the time $t=1$ with step size $h=2.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{W}^{′}(1) & ≈\\frac{W(1+2)-W(1)}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{W(3)-W(1)}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{100-50}{2} \\\\  & =25.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, after $1$ minute, the power is increasing at a rate of approximately $25\\,\\text{watts}/\\text{minute}.$" 13194139535189 true] [17592186092667 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535189 true] [17592186092667 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14057748/assets/5f770f5be6b6131533dc23482281e82024737a4e5a61440dfe1baf5108786381.png)\n\nThe graph of the function $y=g(x)$ is shown above. \nApproximate ${g}^{′}(x)$ at the point $x=6$ using a forward difference approximation with step size $h=4.$" 13194139535189 true] [17592186092667 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/992/Source/Images/q-47386.png)\n\nThe graph of the function $y = g(x)$ is shown above.\nApproximate $g^{′}(x)$ at the point $x = 6$ using a forward difference approximation with step size $h = 4$." 13194139535189 false] [17592186092667 1145 "We use the forward difference approximation\n\n$$\n{g}^{′}(x)≈\\frac{g(x+h)-g(x)}{h}\n$$\n\n \nat the point $x=6.$  Applying the above formula with $x=6$ and $h=4$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}^{′}(6) & ≈\\frac{g(6+4)-g(6)}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{g(10)-g(6)}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{8-2}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{4} \\\\  & =1.5.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535189 true] [17592186336477 1017 #edb/ref 17592186336478 13194139535189 true] [17592186336477 1089 "q-313343" 13194139535189 true] [17592186336477 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139535189 true] [17592186336477 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14057748/assets/204afb23fc438b047c72d6233b59b70c4741ee16a30a57c084682a3d14fd2188.png)\n\nA thin soap film is stretching over time. The graph of its surface area $A(t)$, in square centimeters, after $t$ minutes is shown above. Approximate the rate of change of the surface area at $t=2$ using a forward difference with step size $h=2.$ Do not round the answer.\n\nAfter $2$ minutes, the surface area increases at a rate of {{field-1}} ${\\text{cm}}^{2}/\\text{min}.$" 13194139535189 true] [17592186336477 1113 #uuid "2d82fcd9-f4c5-508c-af92-d0f336d3879f" 13194139535189 true] [17592186336477 1145 "We use the forward difference approximation\n\n$$\n{A}^{′}(t)≈\\frac{A(t+h)-A(t)}{h},\n$$\n\nat the time $t=2$ with step size $h=2.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{′}(2) & ≈\\frac{A(2+2)-A(2)}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{A(4)-A(2)}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{40-10}{2} \\\\  & =15.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, after $2$ minutes, the surface area changes at a rate of approximately $15\\text{ }{\\text{cm}}^{2}/\\text{min}.$" 13194139535189 true] [17592186336478 1000 #edb/ref 17592186336479 13194139535189 true] [17592186336478 1000 #edb/ref 17592186336480 13194139535189 true] [17592186336478 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139535189 true] [17592186336478 1041 "field-1" 13194139535189 true] [17592186336478 1104 #edb/ref 17592186336479 13194139535189 true] [17592186336478 1110 #uuid "6a1f5b66-8134-5091-8a35-8e0a994c04cc" 13194139535189 true] [17592186336479 1034 "15" 13194139535189 true] [17592186336479 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535189 true] [17592186336479 1111 #uuid "9caecc5d-8440-591f-94ab-128cafe9ec7d" 13194139535189 true] [17592186336480 1034 "30" 13194139535189 true] [17592186336480 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535189 true] [17592186336480 1111 #uuid "e59dc82f-546e-5054-bfaf-c42e76ef5fcd" 13194139535189 true] [17592186336481 1017 #edb/ref 17592186336482 13194139535189 true] [17592186336481 1089 "q-341994" 13194139535189 true] [17592186336481 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535189 true] [17592186336481 1100 "A function $g(x)$ has values according to the table below:\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $0.3$ | $0.6$ | $0.9$ | $1.2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $g(x)$ | $-2.7$ | $-1.6$ | $-0.4$ | $1.1$ | $3.7$ |\n\nApproximate ${g}^{′}(x)$ at the point $x=0.6$ using a forward difference approximation. Do not round the answer.\n\n${g}^{′}(0.6)≈$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139535189 true] [17592186336481 1113 #uuid "f572f1e1-b5c8-5dc8-920b-f57ce4ebd415" 13194139535189 true] [17592186336481 1145 "We use the forward difference approximation\n\n$$\n{g}^{′}(x)≈\\frac{g(x+h)-g(x)}{h}\n$$\n\nat the point $x=0.6.$ We choose the step size $h$ to be as small as possible, in this case $h=0.3.$ This gives \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}^{′}(0.6) & ≈\\frac{g(0.6+0.3)-g(0.6)}{0.3} \\\\  & =\\frac{g(0.9)-g(0.6)}{0.3} \\\\  & =\\frac{1.1-(-0.4)}{0.3} \\\\  & =\\frac{1.5}{0.3} \\\\  & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535189 true] [17592186336482 1000 #edb/ref 17592186336483 13194139535189 true] [17592186336482 1000 #edb/ref 17592186336484 13194139535189 true] [17592186336482 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139535189 true] [17592186336482 1041 "field-1" 13194139535189 true] [17592186336482 1104 #edb/ref 17592186336484 13194139535189 true] [17592186336482 1110 #uuid "16a8f30e-e511-558a-8e0a-3dc280f5acd3" 13194139535189 true] [17592186336483 1034 "1.5" 13194139535189 true] [17592186336483 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535189 true] [17592186336483 1111 #uuid "de5c37d3-ec60-55e8-99d0-3c3c22a5d090" 13194139535189 true] [17592186336484 1034 "5" 13194139535189 true] [17592186336484 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535189 true] [17592186336484 1111 #uuid "fbdbd2f5-dc18-51c7-bf03-c9416c70d648" 13194139535189 true] [17592186336485 1017 #edb/ref 17592186336486 13194139535189 true] [17592186336485 1089 "q-341996" 13194139535189 true] [17592186336485 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535189 true] [17592186336485 1100 "A function $g(x)$ has values according to the table below:\n\n| $x$ | $-2$ | $0$ | $2$ | $4$ | $6$ | $8$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $g(x)$ | $20.4$ | $18.6$ | $16.5$ | $14.4$ | $12.3$ | $10.8$ |\n\nApproximate ${g}^{′}(x)$ at the point $x=2$ using a forward difference approximation. Do not round the answer.\n\n${g}^{′}(2)≈$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139535189 true] [17592186336485 1113 #uuid "c1e3efff-e4fc-5ebb-adad-78ad4184693c" 13194139535189 true] [17592186336485 1145 "We use the forward difference approximation\n\n$$\n{g}^{′}(x)≈\\frac{g(x+h)-g(x)}{h}\n$$\n\nat the point $x=2.$ We choose the step size $h$ to be as small as possible, in this case $h=2.$ This gives \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}^{′}(2) & ≈\\frac{g(2+2)-g(2)}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{g(4)-g(2)}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{14.4-16.5}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{-2.1}{2} \\\\  & =-1.05.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535189 true] [17592186336486 1000 #edb/ref 17592186336487 13194139535189 true] [17592186336486 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139535189 true] [17592186336486 1041 "field-1" 13194139535189 true] [17592186336486 1104 #edb/ref 17592186336487 13194139535189 true] [17592186336486 1110 #uuid "f9100525-5208-51f6-afb2-5e3a7429aa6c" 13194139535189 true] [17592186336487 1034 "-1.05" 13194139535189 true] [17592186336487 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535189 true] [17592186336487 1111 #uuid "8c3ef3de-b9a8-5700-a3c5-bc3c04dd32a7" 13194139535189 true] [17592186336488 1017 #edb/ref 17592186336489 13194139535189 true] [17592186336488 1089 "q-342003" 13194139535189 true] [17592186336488 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535189 true] [17592186336488 1100 "A function $d(y)$ has values according to the table below:\n\n| $y$ | $-5$ | $-3$ | $-1$ | $1$ | $3$ | $5$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $d(y)$ | $-8.92$ | $-11.34$ | $-15.23$ | $-17.56$ | $-18.21$ | $-18.50$ |\n\nApproximate ${d}^{′}(y)$ at the point $y=-5$ using a forward difference approximation. Do not round the answer.\n\n${d}^{′}(-5)≈$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139535189 true] [17592186336488 1113 #uuid "8b775af5-e0be-50b8-a8d6-c6f7547ce3b3" 13194139535189 true] [17592186336488 1145 "We use the forward difference approximation\n\n$$\n{d}^{′}(y)≈\\frac{d(y+h)-d(y)}{h}\n$$\n\nat the point $y=-5.$ We choose the step size $h$ to be as small as possible, in this case $h=2.$ This gives \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{d}^{′}(-5) & ≈\\frac{d(-5+2)-d(-5)}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{d(-3)-d(-5)}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{-11.34-(-8.92)}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{-2.42}{2} \\\\  & =-1.21.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535189 true] [17592186336489 1000 #edb/ref 17592186336490 13194139535189 true] [17592186336489 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139535189 true] [17592186336489 1041 "field-1" 13194139535189 true] [17592186336489 1104 #edb/ref 17592186336490 13194139535189 true] [17592186336489 1110 #uuid "1c0ee1a1-21fb-578f-afca-2b23263b2356" 13194139535189 true] [17592186336490 1034 "-1.21" 13194139535189 true] [17592186336490 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535189 true] [17592186336490 1111 #uuid "4b8265b8-d983-5ed0-bc43-002b30c154f9" 13194139535189 true] [17592186336491 1017 #edb/ref 17592186336492 13194139535189 true] [17592186336491 1089 "q-342037" 13194139535189 true] [17592186336491 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535189 true] [17592186336491 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14057748/assets/c51e6206f77b63181f6c9f67b116ba5c0024886c061e8c15fe10edab87c87e03.png)\n\nThe graph of the function $y=f(x)$ is shown above. \nApproximate ${f}^{′}(x)$ at the point $x=3$ using a forward difference approximation with step size $h=2.$ Do not round the answer.\n\n${f}^{′}(3)≈$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139535189 true] [17592186336491 1113 #uuid "9ed1d3b2-7869-51db-81ee-b2ccc32ccf11" 13194139535189 true] [17592186336491 1145 "We use the forward difference approximation\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)≈\\frac{f(x+h)-f(x)}{h}\n$$\n\n \nat the point $x=3.$ Applying the above formula with $x=3$ and $h=2$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(3) & ≈\\frac{f(3+2)-f(3)}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{f(5)-f(3)}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{3-5}{2} \\\\  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535189 true] [17592186336492 1000 #edb/ref 17592186336493 13194139535189 true] [17592186336492 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139535189 true] [17592186336492 1041 "field-1" 13194139535189 true] [17592186336492 1104 #edb/ref 17592186336493 13194139535189 true] [17592186336492 1110 #uuid "9e61e8ba-c70b-5c12-8e33-d8d353588f6e" 13194139535189 true] [17592186336493 1034 "-1" 13194139535189 true] [17592186336493 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535189 true] [17592186336493 1111 #uuid "6456cccc-0cc9-5ecf-92ce-2dbbeede2972" 13194139535189 true] [17592186336494 1017 #edb/ref 17592186336495 13194139535189 true] [17592186336494 1089 "q-342038" 13194139535189 true] [17592186336494 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535189 true] [17592186336494 1100 "The volume of water in a tank was recorded over time for $25$ minutes, starting from time $t=0$ minutes. The following table shows the volume $V(t)$, in liters, recorded at time $t.$\n\n| $t$ | $0$ | $5$ | $10$ | $15$ | $20$ | $25$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $V(t)$ | $120$ | $150$ | $165$ | $180$ | $215$ | $240$ |\n\nApproximate the rate of change of volume at time $t=5$ using a forward difference approximation. Do not round the answer.\n\nAt $t=5,$ the estimated rate of change is {{field-1}} liters per minute." 13194139535189 true] [17592186336494 1113 #uuid "70e8b70f-3021-53a3-b823-74d92203bf17" 13194139535189 true] [17592186336494 1145 "We use the forward difference approximation\n\n$$\n{V}^{′}(t)≈\\frac{V(t+h)-V(t)}{h}\n$$\n\nat the point $t=5.$ We choose the step size $h$ to be as small as possible, in this case $h=5.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{V}^{′}(5) & ≈\\frac{V(5+5)-V(5)}{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{V(10)-V(5)}{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{165-150}{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{15}{5} \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, at $t=5$ the rate of change was approximately $3$ liters per minute." 13194139535189 true] [17592186336495 1000 #edb/ref 17592186336496 13194139535189 true] [17592186336495 1000 #edb/ref 17592186336497 13194139535189 true] [17592186336495 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139535189 true] [17592186336495 1041 "field-1" 13194139535189 true] [17592186336495 1104 #edb/ref 17592186336496 13194139535189 true] [17592186336495 1110 #uuid "b7a6b482-53c5-577a-8e9e-c09454d3673f" 13194139535189 true] [17592186336496 1034 "3" 13194139535189 true] [17592186336496 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535189 true] [17592186336496 1111 #uuid "0f2ba0ab-900f-5b23-afe3-8e9ebaca0f07" 13194139535189 true] [17592186336497 1034 "15" 13194139535189 true] [17592186336497 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535189 true] [17592186336497 1111 #uuid "ba95476f-1be3-51f2-b940-a27e388ae9d3" 13194139535189 true] [17592186336498 1017 #edb/ref 17592186336499 13194139535189 true] [17592186336498 1089 "q-342040" 13194139535189 true] [17592186336498 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535189 true] [17592186336498 1100 "A hiker’s elevation was recorded over time for $6$ hours, starting from time $t=0$ hours. The following table shows the elevation $E(t)$, in meters, recorded at time $t.$\n\n| $t$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ | $4$ | $5$ | $6$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $E(t)$ | $0$ | $200$ | $310$ | $395$ | $450$ | $525$ | $600$ |\n\nApproximate the rate of change of elevation at time $t=1$ using a forward difference approximation. Do not round the answer.\n\nAt $t=1,$ the estimated rate of change is {{field-1}} meters per hour." 13194139535189 true] [17592186336498 1113 #uuid "c44cb097-7152-5126-bd0e-9033f85fd6ed" 13194139535189 true] [17592186336498 1145 "We use the forward difference approximation\n\n$$\n{E}^{′}(t)≈\\frac{E(t+h)-E(t)}{h}\n$$\n\nat the point $t=1.$ We choose the step size $h$ to be as small as possible, in this case $h=1.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}^{′}(1) & ≈\\frac{E(1+1)-E(1)}{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{E(2)-E(1)}{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{310-200}{1} \\\\  & =110.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, at $t=1$ the rate of change was approximately $110$ meters per hour." 13194139535189 true] [17592186336499 1000 #edb/ref 17592186336500 13194139535189 true] [17592186336499 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139535189 true] [17592186336499 1041 "field-1" 13194139535189 true] [17592186336499 1104 #edb/ref 17592186336500 13194139535189 true] [17592186336499 1110 #uuid "45c82cc2-134c-5f6d-b9d2-ea921d99cf28" 13194139535189 true] [17592186336500 1034 "110" 13194139535189 true] [17592186336500 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535189 true] [17592186336500 1111 #uuid "b923956b-e419-5ddd-b919-0bd30634ed97" 13194139535189 true] [17592186336501 1017 #edb/ref 17592186336502 13194139535189 true] [17592186336501 1089 "q-342075" 13194139535189 true] [17592186336501 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139535189 true] [17592186336501 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14057748/assets/d84ba0a07821011be4cf5958af44ea73e97493c5a796a0324b068fe2adca8a12.png)\n\nA car is using petrol as it travels. The graph of the amount of petrol $P(d)$, in liters, remaining in the fuel tank after traveling a distance of $d$ miles is shown above. Approximate the rate of change of the amount of petrol at $d=150$ using a forward difference with step size $h=50.$ Do not round the answer.\n\n*Hint*: Assume that the rate of change is negative if the amount of petrol decreases.\n\nAfter traveling $150$ miles, the rate of change of the amount of petrol is {{field-1}} liters per mile." 13194139535189 true] [17592186336501 1113 #uuid "408ca6b1-3ddb-58f1-8b96-44e416ee959f" 13194139535189 true] [17592186336501 1145 "We use the forward difference approximation\n\n$$\n{P}^{′}(d)≈\\frac{P(d+h)-P(d)}{h},\n$$\n\nat the distance $d=150$ with step size $h=50.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}^{′}(150) & ≈\\frac{P(150+50)-P(150)}{50} \\\\  & =\\frac{P(200)-P(150)}{50} \\\\  & =\\frac{10-30}{50} \\\\  & =-0.4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, after traveling $150$ miles, the rate of change of the amount of petrol is $-0.4$ liters per mile." 13194139535189 true] [17592186336502 1000 #edb/ref 17592186336503 13194139535189 true] [17592186336502 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139535189 true] [17592186336502 1041 "field-1" 13194139535189 true] [17592186336502 1104 #edb/ref 17592186336503 13194139535189 true] [17592186336502 1110 #uuid "257cf450-86c1-5a9d-987c-e360972cb657" 13194139535189 true] [17592186336503 1034 "-0.4" 13194139535189 true] [17592186336503 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535189 true] [17592186336503 1111 #uuid "ca988ad1-34e6-5afc-a468-61832cf2a285" 13194139535189 true] [17592186336504 1017 #edb/ref 17592186336505 13194139535189 true] [17592186336504 1089 "q-342079" 13194139535189 true] [17592186336504 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139535189 true] [17592186336504 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14057748/assets/846fc347c71ada4c01a5a85aabd0722203b84edbafcbe462064ae31d1cf2adbb.png)\n\nA drone is descending toward the ground. The graph of its height $H(t)$, in meters, after $t$ minutes is shown above. Approximate the rate of change of the height at $t=9$ using a forward difference with step size $h=2.$ Do not round the answer.\n\n*Hint*: Assume that the rate of change is negative if the height decreases.\n\nAfter $9$ minutes, the rate of change of the height is {{field-1}} meters per minute." 13194139535189 true] [17592186336504 1113 #uuid "cfa3059e-2dcb-5fa2-abb3-d25cbe930e7c" 13194139535189 true] [17592186336504 1145 "We use the forward difference approximation\n\n$$\n{H}^{′}(t)≈\\frac{H(t+h)-H(t)}{h},\n$$\n\nat the time $t=9$ with step size $h=2.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{H}^{′}(9) & ≈\\frac{H(9+2)-H(9)}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{H(11)-H(9)}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{0-80}{2} \\\\  & =-40.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, after $9$ minutes, the rate of change of the height is $-40$ meters per minute." 13194139535189 true] [17592186336505 1000 #edb/ref 17592186336506 13194139535189 true] [17592186336505 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139535189 true] [17592186336505 1041 "field-1" 13194139535189 true] [17592186336505 1104 #edb/ref 17592186336506 13194139535189 true] [17592186336505 1110 #uuid "5a20db44-2111-5ec2-9b1b-28d00d485b8f" 13194139535189 true] [17592186336506 1034 "-40" 13194139535189 true] [17592186336506 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535189 true] [17592186336506 1111 #uuid "b29d6424-c745-5f0f-9f1d-a47b5c80cbc2" 13194139535189 true] [17592186336507 1017 #edb/ref 17592186336508 13194139535189 true] [17592186336507 1089 "q-342080" 13194139535189 true] [17592186336507 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139535189 true] [17592186336507 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14057748/assets/ae57faa04abd053e4a58d751cc084d58e07e84e0520c99a33a122f58dfd8f18c.png)\n\nA slice of jackfruit is placed in an air dryer. The graph of the moisture content $M(t)$, in percent, after $t$ hours is shown above. Approximate the rate of change of the moisture content at $t=3$ using a forward difference with step size $h=2.$ Do not round the answer.\n\n*Hint*: Assume that the rate of change is negative if the content decreases.\n\nAfter $3$ hour, the rate of change of moisture content is {{field-1}} percent per hour." 13194139535189 true] [17592186336507 1113 #uuid "2642c08d-d846-51fe-9494-87c5a3d1bf87" 13194139535189 true] [17592186336507 1145 "We use the forward difference approximation\n\n$$\n{M}^{′}(t)≈\\frac{M(t+h)-M(t)}{h},\n$$\n\nat the time $t=3$ with step size $h=2.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{M}^{′}(3) & ≈\\frac{M(3+2)-M(3)}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{M(5)-M(3)}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{20-40}{2} \\\\  & =-10.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, after $3$ hour, the rate of change of moisture content is $-10$ percent per hour." 13194139535189 true] [17592186336508 1000 #edb/ref 17592186336509 13194139535189 true] [17592186336508 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139535189 true] [17592186336508 1041 "field-1" 13194139535189 true] [17592186336508 1104 #edb/ref 17592186336509 13194139535189 true] [17592186336508 1110 #uuid "cbeb63ae-4e3e-5c1a-9159-eb9d96349c78" 13194139535189 true] [17592186336509 1034 "-10" 13194139535189 true] [17592186336509 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535189 true] [17592186336509 1111 #uuid "fa218d2d-e025-53fe-ab29-dc986fde4c70" 13194139535189 true] [17592186336510 1017 #edb/ref 17592186336511 13194139535189 true] [17592186336510 1089 "q-47378" 13194139535189 true] [17592186336510 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535189 true] [17592186336510 1100 "Andrey wants to estimate his running speed. Andrey's friend Max takes note of Andrey's position while he is on the track. Max's observations are given in the table below, where $s(t)$ is the distance covered, measured in meters, $t$ seconds after Andrey started running.\n\n| $t$ | $0$ | $2$ | $4$ | $6$ | $8$ | $10$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $s(t)$ | $0$ | $4$ | $9$ | $15$ | $20$ | $27$ |\n\nApproximate Andrey's speed at the point where $t=6$ seconds using a forward difference approximation." 13194139535189 true] [17592186336510 1113 #uuid "e5d32ddd-0763-54da-be13-1a93cdb99dff" 13194139535189 true] [17592186336510 1145 "We use the forward difference approximation\n\n$$\n{s}^{′}(t)≈\\frac{s(t+h)-s(t)}{h}\n$$\n\n \nat the point $t=6.$ We choose the step size $h$ to be as small as possible, in this case $h=2.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{s}^{′}(6) & ≈\\frac{s(6+2)-s(6)}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{s(8)-s(6)}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{20-15}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{2} \\\\  & =2.5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo at $t=6$ Andrey's speed is approximately $2.5\\,\\text{m/s}.$" 13194139535189 true] [17592186336511 1000 #edb/ref 17592186336512 13194139535189 true] [17592186336511 1000 #edb/ref 17592186336513 13194139535189 true] [17592186336511 1000 #edb/ref 17592186336514 13194139535189 true] [17592186336511 1000 #edb/ref 17592186336515 13194139535189 true] [17592186336511 1000 #edb/ref 17592186336516 13194139535189 true] [17592186336511 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535189 true] [17592186336511 1041 "selection" 13194139535189 true] [17592186336511 1104 #edb/ref 17592186336515 13194139535189 true] [17592186336511 1110 #uuid "997ef771-7667-56c3-b41b-61101242d3e1" 13194139535189 true] [17592186336512 1034 "5\\,\\text{m/s}" 13194139535189 true] [17592186336512 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535189 true] [17592186336512 1111 #uuid "b45839c3-7499-59ab-8faa-e0d148ee2a87" 13194139535189 true] [17592186336513 1034 "2\\,\\text{m/s}" 13194139535189 true] [17592186336513 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535189 true] [17592186336513 1111 #uuid "0b0ebc80-d294-5f1b-bd94-0dd7b0bf031e" 13194139535189 true] [17592186336514 1034 "3\\,\\text{m/s}" 13194139535189 true] [17592186336514 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535189 true] [17592186336514 1111 #uuid "d2a88dd3-6060-544d-ba70-4ef6c2975bf9" 13194139535189 true] [17592186336515 1034 "2.5\\,\\text{m/s}" 13194139535189 true] [17592186336515 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535189 true] [17592186336515 1111 #uuid "0832afa2-2713-598b-924f-00733470ebe3" 13194139535189 true] [17592186336516 1034 "3.5\\,\\text{m/s}" 13194139535189 true] [17592186336516 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535189 true] [17592186336516 1111 #uuid "f9e75a82-4cbb-54e8-876d-0ef10e4c